{{also|hithër}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ang|hider}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hidrê}}. Cognate with {{cog|la|citer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈhɪðə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈhɪðɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-hither.ogg|Audio (US)}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɪðə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|literary|or|archaic}} To this place, to here.
#: {{ux|en|He went '''hither''' and thither.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|authorlink=Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=280|passage=But the road left the river again; there were certainly twistings and turnings, as the old woman had said, for at one moment it wound '''hither''' and the next thither, and at some places it was almost imperceptible.}}
# [[over here]]

====Usage notes====
* Compare to the [[pronominal adverb]] "[[hereto]]" which follows the pattern of "preposition + what" or "preposition + which".

====Antonyms====
* [[hence]]

